way. Carrying gets points, but remembering where things go gets double.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll put it away too. You sit down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Fine. I'll count how many times you ask me which cupboard. Those go in my column.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts everyday effort, memory and sacrifice into a running comparison whose scoring rules preserve A's lead.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking repayment for favors.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 explicitly awards double points and A5 assigns cupboard questions to A's column; A wants comparative credit, not a returned favor.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a changes scoring categories when a helpful partner takes over; b reweights time and sacrifice against a coworker's competing contribution.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original's extra trip was physically unclear. The revision keeps shopping and stairs: B2 shifts A toward memory points, and B4's complete takeover makes A5 score consultation instead. Internal reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4 = 92. A1/A3 establish competitive accounting; both offers cause new categories; B4 offers real rest; A5 preserves a lead without doing the task; cupboards make the contest playable; the final column follows the rules A invented.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The explicit points make the mechanism unusually visible; A should sincerely believe the valuation is fair.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S34-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a072f221d4791cef0be694b1d7b6550bd85d2d26f9ab63055cae2075fef32562\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 discounts B's hour and values getting out of bed, clearly seeking the greater sacrifice; A5's claimed sacrifice is less intelligible.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's time comparison, but